WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.458
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.780
We're going to
hear in one evening

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.270
the clarinet played by
many different people

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.100
in many different ways.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.780
We're hearing it
in a jazz setting.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.370
We're hearing it in
an orchestral setting.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:56.020
And I think that gave a nice
opportunity for the audience

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.700
to experience the full
breadth of this instrument.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.700
[MUSIC - INI OGUNTOLA,
 "CASCADES"]

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.450
Third grade is the
earliest age you

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.140
could join the band program.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.200
And so every year they
would show the beginning

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.420
third graders-- they'd
have a presentation, where

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.070
the fourth and fifth graders,
they would go and play

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.050
their instruments on
stage to the third graders

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:29.717
in the audience.

00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:32.080
And then it was trying to
convince them to join the band

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.670
and to pick an instrument.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.370
And so I kind of wanted
to play saxophone

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.565
because of a little bit
of my dad's influence.

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:39.940
That was his
favorite instrument.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.802
But the clarinet
was a lot smaller.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:45.010
And then I realized that it
could fit in my backpack,

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.360
and I wouldn't have to lug
around the heavier saxophone.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.304
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:00.671 --> 00:02:02.643
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:04.615 --> 00:02:07.420
I saw this concert advertised.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.310
Then I saw if you bring your own
instrument, you can play along.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:12.754
And three--

00:02:12.754 --> 00:02:16.170
[CLARINETS PLAYING]

00:02:20.562 --> 00:02:23.680
The fact that we're
inviting clarinetist

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.710
from around town to come
and actually play with us,

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.030
that's kind of unprecedented.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:30.982
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:44.818
I've been learning new notes
throughout the rehearsals.

00:02:44.818 --> 00:02:47.794
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.990
The rhythm to this
piece changes so often

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.933
that it's really fun to play.

00:02:56.933 --> 00:02:58.825
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:02.568 --> 00:03:04.610
To be able to play with
these great clarinetists,

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:08.070
that's just once in a lifetime.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:12.020
[CLARINET SOLO]

